---
import CodeBlock from "@/components/CodeBlock.astro";
import Icon from "@/components/Icon.astro";
import Button from "@/components/ui/Button.astro";
import Docs from "@/layouts/Docs.astro";
import { docHref } from "@/lib/docs";
import { repoFile } from "@/lib/site";
import clientHtml from "../../../apps/sandbox/sample/tasks/client/index.html?raw";
import serverCode from "../../../apps/sandbox/sample/tasks/server/index.ts?raw";
// /sandbox/: the page about CellB sandbox. The sandbox itself (apps/sandbox) is built into the site
// at /sandbox/app/ by scripts/build-sandbox.mjs.
import wrangler from "../../../apps/sandbox/sample/tasks/wrangler.jsonc?raw";

const SANDBOX_APP = "/sandbox/app/";

const layout = `my-app/
├── wrangler.jsonc        the server: a Worker (Durable Objects, KV, D1…)
├── package.json          npm dependencies of both parts
├── server/
│   └── index.ts          wrangler's "main"
└── client/
    ├── index.html        <script type="module" src="./src/main.tsx">
    └── src/              TS / TSX / JS / JSX / CSS`;

const runLocally = `pnpm install
pnpm --filter @statewalker/cellb-sandbox dev     # http://localhost:4321
pnpm --filter @statewalker/cellb-sandbox test    # unit tests (Vitest)
pnpm --filter @statewalker/cellb-sandbox build
pnpm --filter @statewalker/cellb-sandbox e2e     # after build: Chrome, via Playwright`;

const steps = [
  {
    title: "Pick a folder",
    body: "Open a project folder from your disk, or start from the bundled sample. Nothing is uploaded: the files are read in the tab.",
  },
  {
    title: "Compile in the tab",
    body: "The server is bundled by esbuild-wasm. The client's TS, TSX and CSS are compiled module by module when the browser asks for them. npm packages come from the registry.",
  },
  {
    title: "Run it on CellB",
    body: "CellB runs the Worker and its Durable Objects (SQLite WASM in OPFS). A ServiceWorker serves the client and the server together as one site.",
  },
];
---

<Docs
  title="CellB sandbox"
  description="CellB sandbox: open a folder with a Workers server and an HTML client, and run the whole app in your browser tab, compiled and served there."
>
  <p class="font-mono text-sm text-primary">// demo application</p>
  <h1>CellB sandbox</h1>
  <p>
    CellB sandbox runs a full-stack web application from a folder on your computer, inside the
    browser tab. The folder holds a Cloudflare-style Worker (<code>wrangler.jsonc</code>, a Hono
    server, Durable Objects) and an HTML client written in TypeScript or React. The sandbox compiles
    both parts in the tab, runs the server on CellB, and shows the app in a preview frame. No build
    server or container is involved, and the files never leave your machine.
  </p>
  <div class="my-6 flex flex-wrap gap-3">
    <Button href={SANDBOX_APP} size="lg">
      Open CellB sandbox <Icon name="arrowRight" />
    </Button>
    <Button href={repoFile("apps/sandbox/README.md")} variant="outline" size="lg">
      <Icon name="github" /> Source: apps/sandbox
    </Button>
  </div>
  <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground">
    Best in Chrome or Edge: they support the File System Access API, so the sandbox can rerun the
    app when you save a file. Other browsers read the folder once.
  </p>

  <h2>What it does</h2>
  <ol>
    {steps.map((s) => (
      <li>
        <strong>{s.title}.</strong> {s.body}
      </li>
    ))}
  </ol>
  <p>Next to the preview, the sandbox has these tools:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Files</strong> shows the folder's tree and which files are the server and which are the client.</li>
    <li><strong>Log</strong> shows each step with its timing: bundling, startup, the URL the app runs at.</li>
    <li><strong>Rerun on save</strong> watches the folder and restarts the app when a file changes.</li>
    <li><strong>Download .zip</strong> packs the project's files into a ZIP.</li>
    <li><strong>Open in a new tab</strong>: the running app has a URL, and any tab of the site can open it while the sandbox runs.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Durable Object data lives in OPFS in a pool named after the project, so it is still there after a
    rerun or a reload. A rerun downloads nothing again: the worker keeps its npm package store and
    module cache, and rebuilds only the project's own files. In the e2e run the first start of the
    sample takes about 1.9 s, and each rerun about 0.3 s.
  </p>

  <h2>A project folder</h2>
  <p>
    The server part is a normal Workers project. The same folder runs unchanged with
    <code>wrangler dev</code>, celld or Cloudflare. The client part is an HTML page whose
    <code>&lt;script src&gt;</code> and <code>&lt;link href&gt;</code> point to source files.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock lang="txt" title="layout" code={layout} />
  <p>
    The client folder is set by the optional <code>"client"</code> field of
    <code>wrangler.jsonc</code>. Without it, the sandbox uses <code>client/</code>, then
    <code>public/</code>, then the folder's root (an app with no server). The client calls the server
    with <strong>relative</strong> URLs (<code>fetch("api/tasks")</code>), because the sandbox
    serves the app under a path of its own. A deployment at the root of its origin works the same
    way. A request that matches no client file goes to the server.
  </p>

  <h2>The sample: tasks</h2>
  <p>
    "Try the sample" runs a small task list: a Hono server with a <code>TaskList</code> Durable Object
    that stores tasks in its own SQLite database, and a React TSX client. "Download the sample"
    gives the same folder as a ZIP: unpack it, change it, and open it with "Open folder".
  </p>
  <CodeBlock lang="jsonc" title="wrangler.jsonc" code={wrangler.trim()} />
  <CodeBlock lang="ts" title="server/index.ts" code={serverCode.trim()} />
  <CodeBlock lang="html" title="client/index.html" code={clientHtml.trim()} />

  <h2>How it works</h2>
  <p>
    The sandbox is an Astro site with one React island. The work happens in a Dedicated Worker, and
    the page's ServiceWorker serves the result. It is built from CellB's public entry points and the
    packages described in <a href="/docs/from-sources">Running from sources</a>:
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Part</th><th>Done by</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td>Reading the folder</td>
        <td>File System Access API (<code>showDirectoryPicker</code>), else <code>webkitdirectory</code>; changes are found by polling.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Server: bundle</td>
        <td><code>bundleCompiler</code> from <code>@statewalker/cellb/bundle</code>: webrun-bundler on esbuild-wasm, npm packages from the registry.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Server: run</td>
        <td><code>loadProject</code> from <code>@statewalker/cellb/browser</code>: the Worker and its Durable Objects on SQLite WASM in OPFS.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Client: compile</td>
        <td><code>@statewalker/webrun-modules</code>: each TS/TSX/JS/JSX/CSS module is compiled to browser ESM on request, served under <code>/_modules/</code>.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Serving the app</td>
        <td><code>siteHostOverPort</code> (worker) and <code>hostSitesForWorker</code> (page) from <code>@statewalker/cellb/site</code>: the page's ServiceWorker forwards each request to the worker.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>ZIP</td>
        <td><code>zip</code> from <code>@statewalker/cellb-platform-core/util</code>.</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    On this site the sandbox runs at <a href={SANDBOX_APP}><code>{SANDBOX_APP}</code></a>. Its
    ServiceWorker is scoped to that path, so it does not touch the homepage demo's worker. Each
    running app gets a URL of its own below it.
  </p>

  <h2>Run it locally</h2>
  <p>
    The sandbox is the private workspace package <code>@statewalker/cellb-sandbox</code> in
    <a href={repoFile("apps/sandbox")}>apps/sandbox</a>. The site's build builds it with
    <code>SANDBOX_BASE=/sandbox/app/</code> and copies it in
    (<a href={repoFile("site/scripts/build-sandbox.mjs")}>build-sandbox.mjs</a>).
  </p>
  <CodeBlock lang="sh" title="terminal" code={runLocally} />

  <h2>Limits and next steps</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Only <code>&lt;script src&gt;</code> modules are compiled, not inline <code>&lt;script type="module"&gt;</code> blocks.</li>
    <li>The server is re-bundled whole on each run (under a second for the sample). <code>siteCompiler</code> could recompile a single module instead.</li>
    <li>Files are read-only. The next steps toward a CodeSandbox-like tool are an editor that writes back through the folder handle (or to an OPFS copy), a server compiled per module, and a "Deploy" button that pushes the build and the cells' state to a celld bucket.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    More on the pieces it uses: <a href="/docs/from-sources">Running from sources</a>,{" "}
    <a href={docHref("packages/cellb/docs/browser-host.md")}>Running in a browser</a>, and{" "}
    <a href="/examples#live-demos">the live demos</a>.
  </p>
</Docs>
